{"type":"WebSearch","query":"California 2024 election ballot rejection signature mismatch statistics","content":"**In California's November 5, 2024, general election, 71,387 vote-by-mail (VBM) ballots were rejected specifically for non-matching signatures, representing about 58% of all rejected VBM ballots.** This was the most common rejection reason. Overall, 122,439 VBM ballots were rejected (0.92% of the 13,274,125 returned), with 13,151,689 accepted (99.08%). Other major reasons included late arrival (32,935 or ~27%) and no signature (13,356 or ~11%). These figures come from California Secretary of State data as of December 13, 2024 (via VoteCal); totals can shift slightly as signature issues are cured. Later reports cite ~122,480 total rejections.[[1]](https://elections.cdn.sos.ca.gov/vbm-rejection-reason/2024/november-general-2024.pdf)

Voters can typically cure missing or mismatched signatures by submitting a verification statement (by mail, in person, fax, or email) up to two days before certification. Roughly 197,000 voters were initially notified of signature problems (missing or unmatched); about 114,000 were cured, leaving ~83,000 uncounted (aligning closely with the combined missing + mismatch totals). Young voters (18-24) faced the highest overall VBM rejection rates (~3.3%, often due to mismatches) and lower cure rates. Rejection rates also varied by county (e.g., Imperial ~1.94–2.5%, Amador 0.17%) and demographics, with higher signature-issue rates among Latino and younger voters.[[2]](https://www.sacbee.com/news/politics-government/capitol-alert/article302379904.html)

These statistics apply to VBM ballots (used by ~81% of voters); in-person ballots have different processes. Signature verification compares the envelope signature to voter records (sometimes using software plus human review). Primary election (March 2024) data showed higher overall rejections (~1.56%, with 23,523 for mismatches). Official reports and analyses (e.g., USC Center for Inclusive Democracy) confirm signature issues as the leading cause, with some variation in application across counties. For the latest or county-specific details, check the California Secretary of State’s VBM rejection reports.","citations":["https://elections.cdn.sos.ca.gov/vbm-rejection-reason/2024/november-general-2024.pdf","https://www.sacbee.com/news/politics-government/capitol-alert/article302379904.html"],"allowed_domains":null}
